Replaced the per-element FDOT10.S (0.24 MAC/word, 3,000,000 invocations, 1.13x) with FGEMM10.S, one custom-0 instruction (funct3=000, funct7=0000000). It computes the entire 10x10x10 binary32 matrix multiply that the profiled multiply() performs (99.5% of cycles). Operands: in[0..99]=A and in[100..199]=B, both row-major, which matches Matrix.elements. out[0..99]=C, out[100..199]=0, and rd=C[0][0]. Each MAC is a separately rounded RNE mul then add starting from +0.0 with k ascending, so results are bit-identical to the non-fused software. NaN results are canonicalized with a mask select, and trip counts are fixed, so the instruction is constant time. Declared: words=200, mac_ops=1000 (2.5 MAC per word moved, 10x the old design), invocations=30000 (one per multiply call in the 30,000-iteration harness), marshal_words=300 (200 copied in, 100 copied back to C). Expected cost is about 2230 instruction cycles plus about 7700 marshalling cycles, against about 80,000 software cycles per multiply. That is roughly 8x on the kernel and about 7-8x whole-application, well above the 1.32x bar. Non-10x10 or aliased cases fall back to the original software loop.